## The Shades to Avoid, and Why They Look Off on Medium Complexions

Knowing what not to wear is as useful as knowing what works. And for medium skin, the answers are specific enough to be genuinely actionable.

**Cool-pink nudes.** Any nude with a dominant pink-blue undertone, OPI Bubble Bath is the canonical example, will grey out on medium skin. The formula is designed to complement cool, fair complexions. On warm medium skin, it reads ashy. Skip anything described as "sheer pink," "ballet pink," or "blush" unless you can confirm a warm base.

**Icy pastels.** Cool mint, baby blue, lavender with grey-purple undertones, on medium skin, these read stark and slightly sickly. The contrast is jarring rather than fresh. A warm-shifted version of the same family works; the cool version does not.

**Blue-based reds.** A true blue-red (think classic crimson or raspberry red) can read too cool against warm medium skin. The warmth of your complexion fights the cool in the formula, and neither wins. A red with orange warmth, as discussed in the coral and warm red section, is the medium-skin version of the same impulse done right.

**Very light, flat pastels at sheer coverage.** These simply disappear. Medium skin needs either saturation, depth, or a finish that catches light. A flat pastel applied thinly does none of those things. <a href='https://www.palettehunt.com/beauty/nail-color-for-tan-skin' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>PaletteHunt's tan skin nail research</a> identifies this as the single most common shade mistake made by medium-to-tan skin readers, and it is the one most easily avoided once you know to look for it.

